WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
Speaker 1: So we had some&nbsp;
major media news yesterday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:07.680
many of you emailing me about it, tweeting to&nbsp;
me about it, sending me very, very respectful&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:09.840
Speaker 2: of sorts
Speaker 1: of messages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:16.320
about it. The story is Chris Wallace leaving&nbsp;
Fox News and joining CNN plus CNN Plus being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:23.600
a new streaming service from CNN. And this&nbsp;
is a really big deal in the political media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.720
space because Chris Wallace, as many of you&nbsp;
know, was one of what we would call the lone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:35.840
occasional voices of reason on Fox News, which&nbsp;
was basically a sea of propaganda. And I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.040
had a number of different thoughts about Chris&nbsp;
Wallace presence on Fox in the past, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.320
there's no better time to talk about that now. But&nbsp;
let's first take a look at Chris Wallace making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.160
the announcement yesterday on Fox News Sunday
Speaker 3: after 18 years. This is my final Fox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:58.880
News Sunday. It is the last time and I say this&nbsp;
with real sadness we will meet like this. Eighteen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.080
years ago, the bosses here at Fox promised me they&nbsp;
would never interfere with a guest I booked or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:10.880
question I asked. And they kept that promise. I&nbsp;
have been free to report to the best of my ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.440
to cover the stories I think are important&nbsp;
to hold our country's leaders to account.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.880
It's been a great ride. We've&nbsp;
covered five presidential elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:27.200
interviewed every president since George H.W. Bush&nbsp;
traveled the world, sitting down with France's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:34.160
Emmanuel Macron and Russia's Vladimir Putin. And&nbsp;
I've gotten to spend Sunday mornings with you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:40.560
It may sound corny, but I feel we built a&nbsp;
community here. There's a lot you can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.240
on Sunday mornings. The fact you've chosen to&nbsp;
spend this hour with us is something I cherish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:53.760
But after 18 years, I have decided to leave Fox.&nbsp;
I want to try something new to go beyond politics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.640
to all the things I'm interested in. I'm ready&nbsp;
for a new adventure and I hope you'll check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:06.400
it out. And so for the last time, dear friends.
Speaker 1: OK, so he's leaving. He said he has new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:13.760
projects, and then within two hours, CNN announced&nbsp;
he's going to be joining as an anchor of their new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.560
streaming service. CNN Plus, which&nbsp;
doesn't exist yet, it's expected to debut&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.429
Speaker 2: early next year.
Speaker 1: So is this good or is this bad? And for&nbsp;

00:02:23.429 --> 00:02:26.480
Speaker 2: who or whom, depending on
Speaker 1: your preference?&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.920
Speaker 2: On the one hand, it's been clear
Speaker 1: for a long time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:37.280
that Chris Wallace Days Fox were&nbsp;
probably numbered if he was going to keep&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.960
Speaker 2: asking just
Speaker 1: real&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.880
Speaker 2: questions of Republicans.
Speaker 1: When Republicans would be on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.680
and we had all of these different examples of&nbsp;
I don't even remember what Senator it was, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:53.520
was talking about debt. And Chris Wallace pointed&nbsp;
out, well, a lot of that debt accrued under Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.760
not under Joe Biden. And you know, this is&nbsp;
flexing in other countries. We would call it basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.920
journalism. It was rare on Fox and because Chris&nbsp;
Wallace was doing it and he was doing it with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.829
Republicans as well as Democrats. It was rare it&nbsp;
stood out and there was some sense that at some&nbsp;

00:03:09.829 --> 00:03:14.080
Speaker 2: point he might be gone.
Speaker 1: Now, on the one hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:22.160
you could say his mere presence on Fox was a&nbsp;
net positive because there were some viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.720
who were fully accepting all the propaganda.
Speaker 2: And even if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.400
didn't care that this is what
Speaker 1: Chris Wallace was doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.400
they would be exposed to it for a moment.&nbsp;
And maybe he would change some minds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.720
get some of these mindless Fox viewers to&nbsp;
think, Hey, here's another perspective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:45.280
The counterpoint to that is that Chris&nbsp;
Wallace did provide Fox News cover&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.400
Speaker 2: in a major way to be able
Speaker 1: to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.200
We're fair and balanced. We have
Speaker 2: news and we have&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.320
Speaker 1: opinion. That's one they've been&nbsp;
hiding behind for a very long time. It's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.400
yes, you have news and opinion. It's blurred.&nbsp;
And even the news sounds like opinion.&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.349
Speaker 2: And we have plenty of people
Speaker 1: holding Republicans&nbsp;

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:07.040
Speaker 2: accountable, like Chris Wallace.
Speaker 1: They used to have Shep Smith, although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:13.840
Shep Smith left, and in that sense, it provided
Speaker 2: cover for Fox&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.360
Speaker 1: News to be marginally more plausible
Speaker 2: as an actual news channel&nbsp;

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:24.640
Speaker 1: of sorts, with actual journalism&nbsp;
being done. And so with Chris Wallace leaving,&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.000
Speaker 2: does it
Speaker 1: prevent Fox from now&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.629
Speaker 2: having that cover?
Speaker 1: My bottom line on this is as follows I&nbsp;

00:04:32.629 --> 00:04:37.760
Speaker 2: think it's a you've got two sides of
Speaker 1: the scale and they're they're really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:43.280
both doing work. It's close, right? On the one&nbsp;
hand, his presence is good as an alternative to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:49.760
the propaganda. On the other hand, his presence&nbsp;
allows Fox to run interference. My expectation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.880
is that now Chris Wallace will be replaced&nbsp;
with a total Partizan hack. People on Twitter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.720
we're talking about Dana Perino. Dana Perino,&nbsp;
who's a Fox anchor now, was George W. Bush's press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:07.200
secretary, Robin. Give me a break, guys.
Speaker 2: So he'll probably&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.360
Speaker 1: be replaced with someone
Speaker 2: much worse.&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:16.160
Speaker 1: I think that by a small&nbsp;
margin, small margin. Wallace on Fox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:23.840
was a net positive. He did ask real questions&nbsp;
and maybe convinced some people. He did&nbsp;

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.840
Speaker 2: allow Fox to say, We have a
Speaker 1: variety and we have journalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:35.760
and we have Chris Wallace. I think the the benefit&nbsp;
of having him there slightly slightly outweighed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:42.240
the very pernicious reality that he was running&nbsp;
interference for what was otherwise a complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.389
and total propaganda media outlet.
Speaker 2: And in&nbsp;

00:05:46.389 --> 00:05:51.360
Speaker 1: particular, if he now gets replaced
Speaker 2: with Tucker or Hannity wannabe&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.000
Speaker 1: not not literally&nbsp;
Tucker, but a Tucker or Hannity&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.520
Speaker 2: Tucker or Hannity
Speaker 1: wannabe. I think we are worse off.&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.000
Speaker 2: And so the
Speaker 1: criticism that he ran interference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:07.680
for a horrible propaganda network is 100 percent&nbsp;
accurate. And I would say by like five percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:26.560
him being there was a net benefit. Do you&nbsp;
disagree with me? I want to hear from him

